hello and welcome to the new world  review your source for everything anime  and manga and today demon slayer just  demon saya  so I've recently watched episode 19 and  I don't know what this video is it's not  like a review or any kind of analysis  it's more of a stream of consciousness  and yes I know I'm very late to this  party but I also don't really care now a  whole ton of you commented on episode 19  in my first impressions video hoping  that I would make it that far and  telling me that it would somewhat blow  my mind and you guys weren't exactly  wrong which is particularly impressive  because after all of the comments I went  into episode nineteen expecting  something pretty big to happen and what  I saw still very much exceeded my  expectations and look initially what I  wanted to do was time to this video  something like the greatest fight in  anime history you know something nice  and clickbait tea but also arguably true  because that sort of thing is almost  entirely subjective anyway however when  I took a step back I realized such title  would be doing episode 19 and grave  injustice because it's not just about  the fight because this entire episode  was just beautifully constructed  allowing that one particular moment to  shine and the key thing that allows  everything to work is one word that word  being family having come this far into  the series I'd begun to realize that  part of the charm Demon Slayer presents  is through this concept because it's  actually very rarely explored in a  shounen series many of which featured a  young male protagonist with no familial  ties presumably to make their call to  adventure much easier to accept and to  not have too many realistic  complications bulking down an  action-adventure story but what many  writers would see as a narrative  restriction demon slayer chooses to put  great emphasis on and in my opinion is a  core factor that has allowed episode 19  to transcend itself because here's the  thing on paper this episode should have  infuriated me and that's because our  young and still relatively inexperienced  protagonist managed to get the better of  one of the twelve Kazuki out of demon  moons whatever you prefer  but in any case they are a group that  have been built up thus far to be second  only to Michael Jackson demon himself  there is nothing that should have  allowed tandro to overcome such a beast  at his current level and anything that  did would be poor portion and  storytelling at least that's what I  would have thought before seeing this  episode in fact during the entire thing  I was expecting you to show up and save  tandro and that only actually stopped  when tandra engaged in his he no kami  thing go and that's when I went no he's  not actually  going to beat him is he that would be  one of the biggest ass pulls in anime  history surely of course at the time I  really didn't care if that was going to  be the case because my god this anime is  just so stunning the fire animation is  every bit as mesmerizing as the water I  love so much and they've done a  fantastic job of making it feel more  volatile and just plain more powerful  which allows me to accept what's  happening even if my brain doesn't feel  quite right about it but once again on  paper this is a simple powerup  it's a classic Shonen protagonist comes  up against an insurmountable power and  has to rapidly evolve during battle to  overcome it except that it isn't because  there's another complication that it's  not just one powerup it's two because  the other part of this equation that my  mind had all but forgotten until now was  Nezzer ku thus far she spent the series  being tones Rose driving force and  somewhat relevant to combat but nothing  approaching what happens here when she  activates her blood demon art after that  happened I felt entirely satisfied  there's just something so wonderful in  gaining power through familial bonds  because tandra achieved this assumedly  through their father austere Zuko is  awakened by their mother and then it's  like the bonds of a whole family coming  together to overcome ruie which is just  such poetic justice as well because rui  has spent his demonic career trying to  mimic these bonds only to be beaten by  the very thing that he seeks and at this  time I should also point out that the  anime doesn't deserve complete credit  for this because it was assumedly all  faithfully adapted from the manga where  these wonderful ideas were laid out  however the way it was portrayed in  motion to me epitomizes my love for this  medium and the absurd amount of  potential it has to elevate an already  stunning story when implemented  correctly and I may still be on a high  from just having watched it recently but  I would legitimately call the final two  minutes of episode 19 one of my favorite  anime moments of all time everything  about it is perfect from the motion of  the movement which slides skillfully  between spectacularly fast and  spellbinding ly slow and you know what  it almost comes across as a perfectly  choreographed dance with the music and  the music my god here's another thing  that out of context I never would have  thought would work so well if you'd  played me that song and told me look  this is what we're going to use for the  most climactic battle of the series so  far and I would have severely questioned  your sanity however in this context it  works so damn well because it's more  subdued and emotionally focused nature  only goes to serve the idea of family  and add that intimate energy which is  very much needed to make this moment  100% convincing plus what the song lacks  in  if you're on fizz more than made up for  with the sound effects of the action on  screen which more often than not  followed their own musical nature so  there's this incredible juxtaposition of  soft warmth with desperate action that  really should not work anywhere near as  well as it does but of course my  favorite moment is the impact this is a  masterclass of how object should collide  in anime both the force and resistance  look perfect the sound is glorious and  the particle animation really sells the  sheer power emanating from the strike  making it all the more believable when a  Zuko's blood demon art goes off again  and results in the decapitation and you  know then of course there's this moment  of beauty in all of my many many years I  have never seen a more stunning  decapitation and after this I had to  immediately go to the manga I needed to  know exactly how much of this action was  mandated by what was on the page and  what the anime had taken into its own  hands to elevate and the answer is after  seeing episode 19 I was honestly  disappointed with what I saw in what I  think was chapter 40 of the manga and  you might be saying that it's probably  really not fair to compare the manga to  any anime adaptation but there are  certainly series out there whose manga  greatly outshines its anime counterpart  from what I've seen I don't think this  is one of those circumstances but you  know what it's not fair to judge the  manga based solely on the comparison  with this godlike anime scene and having  gotten this far into the series I do  plan on reading the manga once the anime  has concluded still this episode is  without a doubt a crowning achievement  of 2019 and full credit to you photo  ball for crafting this masterwork and  that pretty much does it for whatever  this was if you enjoyed this video and  the content this channel produced in  general and please do consider donating  to the New World review patreon because  the support of all of your amazing  people is what continues to make this  channel possible and if patreon isn't  quite your style then please do leave  this video a like share or subscribe  because it also helps support this  channel an incredible amount and if  you'd like to join the fun at any time  then please do head over to my discord  server where a wide array of shenanigan  area takes place on a daily basis and  finally please do comment with your  thoughts on demon slayer episode 19 this  has been the new old review and I'll see  you next time  [Music]

